---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Shot from '../components/Shot.astro';
import Feature from '../components/Feature.astro';

const jump = [
  ['#library', 'Library'],
  ['#streaming', 'Streaming'],
  ['#discover', 'Discover'],
  ['#live', 'Live TV &amp; DVR'],
  ['#audio', 'Music &amp; audio'],
  ['#organize', 'Collections'],
  ['#ai', 'AI'],
  ['#requests', 'Requests'],
  ['#household', 'Profiles'],
  ['#personal', 'Personalize'],
  ['#roadmap', 'Coming soon'],
];
---

<Base
  title="Features — what the Vora app does"
  description="A tour of the Vora app: library browsing and playback, discovery and release calendar, live TV with a full guide, music with synced lyrics, podcasts, internet radio, collections and playlists, profiles with parental controls, and per-profile themes."
>
  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="shell">
      <span class="eyebrow">Features</span>
      <h1 style="font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.2rem);">What the app does</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Everything below is the part you actually use — browsing, watching and listening. If you are
        looking for scanning, plugins and deployment, that lives on the
        <a href="/server">server page</a>.
      </p>

      <nav class="surface-nav" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;" aria-label="Sections">
        {jump.map(([href, label]) => <a href={href} set:html={label} />)}
      </nav>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="surface" id="library">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="spotlight">
        <div class="spotlight__body">
          <span class="pill">Library</span>
          <h3 style="margin-top: 1rem;">Browse a collection that looks after itself</h3>
          <p>
            Each library opens on a mosaic of its own artwork with a live count of what is inside. Filter
            and sort the grid, jump by letter, or switch to Collections and For You. Posters carry the
            things you care about at a glance — 4K, Dolby Vision, HDR10, TrueHD, DTS, the TMDB score and
            the Rotten Tomatoes percentage.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Shot src="web/library" url="vora.local/library" alt="The Vora library grid with filters and badged posters" />
      </div>

      <div class="spotlight spotlight--reverse">
        <div class="spotlight__body">
          <span class="pill">Detail</span>
          <h3 style="margin-top: 1rem;">Cast, crew and every episode</h3>
          <p>
            Detail pages lead with the backdrop, then the cast rail with character names, then the
            episodes — each with its own still, air date, runtime and summary.
          </p>
          <p>
            Vora analyses each file for silence and black frames and clusters the results across a whole
            season, so <strong>Skip Intro</strong>, <strong>Skip Recap</strong> and
            <strong>Skip Credits</strong> appear exactly when they should — and the seekbar paints the
            bands so you can see them coming. Each profile decides whether intros and credits skip on
            their own. Mid- and post-credits scenes never auto-skip; you get a
            <strong>Skip to Scene</strong> button instead, because skipping a stinger is not a
            convenience. Markers can be edited by hand and locked so the next scan leaves them alone.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Shot src="web/show-season" url="vora.local/shows" alt="A season detail page with cast rail and episode list" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="surface" id="streaming">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Streaming</span>
        <h2>How playback is decided</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Most of what separates a pleasant self-hosted library from an annoying one happens in the
          half-second between pressing play and the picture appearing. Here is what Vora does in it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="spotlight spotlight--wide">
        <div class="spotlight__body" style="max-width: 74ch;">
          <h3>The client measures itself first</h3>
          <p>
            Rather than assuming what a device can handle from its user agent, each client probes its own
            decoders and reports the result: which of H.264, HEVC, VP9 and AV1 actually decode; which of
            AAC, AC3, E-AC3, Opus, FLAC and MP3 it accepts; which containers it will open; how many audio
            channels are available; whether the screen can render HDR10, HLG or Dolby Vision; and whether
            the decoder is 10-bit or 8-bit.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two details that matter in practice. Android only claims HEVC when both the 8-bit and 10-bit
            decoders exist, because a decoder that advertises HEVC and then chokes on a 10-bit file is
            worse than one that never claimed it. And on Android TV, Vora detects when the connected
            receiver can bitstream AC3, E-AC3 or DTS — so a surround track is passed through to the amp
            instead of being quietly downmixed to stereo.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="spotlight spotlight--wide" style="margin-top: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 4rem);">
        <div class="spotlight__body" style="max-width: 74ch;">
          <h3>The server scores every option it has</h3>
          <p>
            Vora does not look at one file and decide yes or no. It takes every copy of the title you own
            and every audio track inside each one, scores all of those combinations against what your
            device just told it, and picks the cheapest path that will genuinely play. Video and audio
            are decided separately, so a file with one unsupported audio track gets its video copied
            straight through while only the sound is re-encoded.
          </p>
          <p>
            The scoring has opinions. A 4K source scaled down beats a native 720p copy, because the
            picture is better. The closest-fitting source wins over a needlessly large one. The default
            audio track gets a nudge — and a track with &ldquo;commentary&rdquo; in its name gets pushed
            firmly down the list, because nobody means to start a film on the director's commentary.
          </p>
          <p class="note">
            When it does transcode, the output is H.264 and AAC over HLS — the combination essentially
            everything can play. Transcoding an HDR source tone-maps it to SDR, and the player badge and
            history record what you were actually served rather than what the file claims, so nothing
            says 4K HDR while you are watching 1080p SDR.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="paths">
        <div class="path">
          <span class="path__label">Best case</span>
          <h3>Direct play</h3>
          <p>Container and codecs all supported. The file goes over untouched.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="path">
          <span class="path__label">Next best</span>
          <h3>Remux</h3>
          <p>Codecs fine, container wrong. Repackaged into MP4, no frames re-encoded.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="path">
          <span class="path__label">Last resort</span>
          <h3>Transcode</h3>
          <p>Something truly cannot play. GPU-accelerated where hardware allows.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="spotlight" style="margin-top: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 4rem);">
        <div class="spotlight__body">
          <h3>You get a say too</h3>
          <p>
            Playback settings are per profile <em>and</em> per device, so capping your phone on mobile
            data does not touch the television. Set a maximum bitrate, a maximum resolution and a
            channel limit; a preference can only ever ask for less than the hardware can do, never more.
            The server can separately cap remote streams so someone watching from a hotel does not
            saturate your upload.
          </p>
          <p>
            An administrator picks the house priority between three profiles —
            <strong>Client Preference</strong>, <strong>Direct Stream Preference</strong>, which avoids
            transcoding almost at any cost, and <strong>Bandwidth Optimized</strong>, which trades
            picture for upload headroom and caps each resolution to a bitrate ceiling.
          </p>
          <div class="btn-row">
            <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/server#streaming">The administrator's side of this</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <Shot src="admin/dashboard" url="vora.local/admin" alt="The admin dashboard showing active streams and their playback strategy" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="surface" id="discover">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Discover</span>
        <h2>Finding the next thing</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Three different answers to &ldquo;what now?&rdquo; — what the world is watching, what you already
          own and have not started, and what is about to arrive.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="spotlight">
        <div class="spotlight__body">
          <h3>Out in the world</h3>
          <p>
            Popular, upcoming and curated rows from TMDB and MyAnimeList, with anything already on your
            server flagged <em>In library</em> so you never chase something you own. Bookmark the rest to
            your watchlist, and pick which rows appear.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Shot src="web/discovery" url="vora.local/discover" alt="The Discover page with popular and upcoming rows from TMDB" />
      </div>

      <div class="spotlight spotlight--reverse">
        <div class="spotlight__body">
          <h3>Already yours</h3>
          <p>
            For You builds rows out of your own shelves — &ldquo;Because you like Animation&rdquo;, top-rated
            and unwatched — so a library that has grown past browsing still surfaces something worth
            starting.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Shot src="web/library-for-you" url="vora.local/library" alt="For You recommendation rows drawn from the user's library" />
      </div>

      <div class="spotlight">
        <div class="spotlight__body">
          <h3>Still to come</h3>
          <p>
            A release calendar in month, week or day view covers what is landing next, and the watchlist
            keeps everything you flagged in one place across every device.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Shot src="web/release-calendar" url="vora.local/calendar" alt="The release calendar in month view" />
      </div>

      <div class="gallery" style="margin-top: 3rem;">
        <Shot src="web/watchlist" url="vora.local/watchlist" alt="The watchlist page" caption="Watchlist" />
        <Shot src="web/global-collections" url="vora.local/collections" alt="Global collections across libraries" caption="Collections across libraries" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="surface" id="live">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="spotlight spotlight--wide">
        <div class="spotlight__body" style="max-width: 72ch;">
          <span class="pill">Live TV</span>
          <h3 style="margin-top: 1rem;">A guide built for actually watching television</h3>
          <p>
            Bring your own IPTV playlists and EPG sources and Vora turns them into one grid: categories
            with counts, region and resolution filters, channel search, favourites, and a preview window
            that plays the current programme while you keep browsing. Pause and rewind live TV, and
            schedule a recording for an episode or a whole series from inside the player.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <Shot src="web/live-tv-guide" url="vora.local/live" alt="The full live TV guide grid with preview and category filters" />

      <div class="gallery" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
        <Shot src="web/live-tv" url="vora.local/live" alt="The live TV channel list" caption="Channels" />
        <Shot src="web/live-radio-and-tv-provider-access" url="vora.local/settings" alt="Per-profile provider access for live TV and radio" caption="Per-profile provider access" />
      </div>

      <div class="spotlight spotlight--wide" style="margin-top: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);">
        <div class="spotlight__body" style="max-width: 74ch;">
          <span class="pill">DVR</span>
          <h3 style="margin-top: 1rem;">Record it and watch it later</h3>
          <p>
            Open a programme in the guide and you get three choices: play the channel,
            <strong>record this episode</strong>, or <strong>record the series</strong>. A series
            recording takes a retention rule — keep everything, or only the latest 5, 10 or 20 — and
            Vora schedules each new episode as the guide predicts it, dropping the oldest when the limit
            is reached. Cancel one and it asks whether you meant this episode or the whole series.
          </p>
          <p>
            Recordings live on the <strong>DVR</strong> tab beside the guide, grouped by programme and
            split into Completed, Upcoming and Failed. A finished recording that is still being processed
            says so rather than playing you a half-written file. Where Comskip has flagged the ad breaks,
            a <strong>Skip commercial</strong> button appears while you are inside one — offered, never
            automatic.
          </p>
          <p>
            Live television can also be paused. With timeshift permission, a channel gets a real scrub
            bar, skip back ten seconds and skip forward thirty — the same controls you would expect from
            a set-top box, on a stream that is still going out live.
          </p>
          <p class="muted">
            Recording and timeshift are separate per-user permissions, and DVR users can be given a
            storage quota so one person cannot fill the disk.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="surface" id="audio">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Music, podcasts &amp; radio</span>
        <h2>The whole audio side</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Not a bolted-on music tab — a full audio app that keeps playing in the background, on the lock
          screen, and in the car.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="spotlight">
        <div class="spotlight__body">
          <h3>Music</h3>
          <p>
            Artists, albums, genres, liked songs, top tracks and daily mixes, with a full-screen player
            that tints itself from the album art. Lyrics scroll in time with the track, the queue is one
            tap away, and crossfade, an equalizer and a sleep timer are built in.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Shot src="web/music-player-with-lyrics" url="vora.local/music" alt="The music player with time-synced lyrics" />
      </div>

      <div class="gallery" style="margin-top: 3rem;">
        <Shot src="web/music-home" url="vora.local/music" alt="The music home screen" caption="Music home" />
        <Shot src="web/album" url="vora.local/music" alt="An album page with its track list" caption="Album" />
        <Shot src="web/music-player-plain" url="vora.local/music" alt="The music player without lyrics" caption="Player" />
      </div>

      <div class="spotlight" style="margin-top: 4rem;">
        <div class="spotlight__body">
          <h3>Podcasts</h3>
          <p>
            Search the directory, subscribe, and get new episodes as they land. Position is remembered
            per episode and follows you between devices.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Shot src="web/podcast-player" url="vora.local/podcasts" alt="The podcast episode player" />
      </div>

      <div class="spotlight spotlight--reverse" style="margin-top: 4rem;">
        <div class="spotlight__body">
          <h3>Internet radio</h3>
          <p>
            Stations browse like a library, favourites come first, and because a station list plays as a
            real queue, previous and next skip between stations — on the phone and on the head unit
            alike.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Shot src="web/internet-radio-player" url="vora.local/radio" alt="The internet radio player" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="surface" id="organize">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Collections &amp; playlists</span>
        <h2>Put things in order</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="grid">
        <Feature title="Collections" icon="grid">
          Group a franchise or a universe, sync the contents from a Trakt, MDbList or IMDb list, and let
          it order itself chronologically rather than by release date.
        </Feature>
        <Feature title="Playlists" icon="music">
          Hand-built playlists for video or audio, reorderable, shareable across your profiles.
        </Feature>
        <Feature title="Smart playlists" icon="sliders">
          A rule tree — match all or any, per-type fields, live preview — so &ldquo;unwatched 4K comedies
          over 100 minutes&rdquo; stays correct as the library changes.
        </Feature>
      </div>

      <div class="gallery" style="margin-top: 3rem;">
        <Shot src="web/library-collections" url="vora.local/library" alt="Collections inside a library" caption="Library collections" />
        <Shot src="web/global-collections" url="vora.local/collections" alt="Collections spanning libraries" caption="Global collections" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="surface" id="ai">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">AI</span>
        <h2>AI, where it actually saves you an afternoon</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Vora does not have a chatbot bolted to the side. It uses a model for three specific jobs that
          are otherwise tedious hand work — and every one of them is off until you turn it on and supply
          your own OpenAI key.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="spotlight">
        <div class="spotlight__body">
          <h3>Name a universe, get the collection</h3>
          <p>
            Set a collection's list provider to <strong>AI List</strong> and type what you want in plain
            language — &ldquo;Every movie and show for Marvel Cinematic Universe&rdquo;. Vora works out
            what belongs, pulls in every film and season, and rechecks on the cadence you set. Tick
            <em>mirror the list exactly</em> and anything that drops off the list leaves the collection
            too.
          </p>
          <p class="muted">
            This is for franchises and shared universes. For &ldquo;all my kung-fu movies&rdquo;, a smart
            collection with rules is the better tool and costs nothing.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Shot
          src="web/ai-collection-list-filler"
          frame="plain"
          alt="The collection editor with AI List selected and a franchise described in plain language"
        />
      </div>

      <div class="spotlight spotlight--reverse">
        <div class="spotlight__body">
          <h3>Watch it in the order the story happens</h3>
          <p>
            Release order and story order are rarely the same thing, and working out the difference by
            hand is a weekend lost to forum arguments. Pick <strong>AI Chronological Order</strong>,
            describe how it should run, and the timeline becomes a sort option viewers can switch to —
            with auto-sync filing anything new into the right slot rather than onto the end.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Shot
          src="web/ai-collection-chronological-ordering"
          frame="plain"
          alt="The chronological ordering panel with AI Chronological Order selected and auto-sync enabled"
        />
      </div>

      <div class="spotlight">
        <div class="spotlight__body">
          <h3>Recommendations that read the whole shelf</h3>
          <p>
            With the recommendations plugin enabled, Vora embeds your library and uses it to build the
            suggestion rows. Leave it off and For You still works — it falls back to the local engine,
            which ranks by what you have not watched and how well it scored.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Shot
          src="web/library-for-you"
          url="vora.local/library"
          alt="For You recommendation rows"
        />
      </div>

      <div class="spotlight spotlight--reverse">
        <div class="spotlight__body">
          <h3>Every token, itemised</h3>
          <p>
            Because it is your key and your bill, the server keeps a log: which feature made the call,
            which profile triggered it, which model answered, how many prompt and completion tokens it
            used, and what it is estimated to have cost. Filter by date or by feature. Nothing runs up a
            bill quietly in the background.
          </p>
          <div class="btn-row">
            <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/server">More on the server side</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <Shot
          src="admin/ai-usage"
          url="vora.local/admin"
          alt="The AI usage and cost page with token counts and a per-call log"
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="surface" id="requests">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="spotlight spotlight--wide">
        <div class="spotlight__body" style="max-width: 74ch;">
          <span class="pill">Requests</span>
          <h3 style="margin-top: 1rem;">The watchlist is the request button</h3>
          <p>
            Most media servers make asking for something its own chore: a separate app, a separate login,
            a form. Vora folds it into the thing people already do. When a viewer adds a title to their
            watchlist and that title is not in the library, it becomes a request automatically — no
            second interface, no explaining to your family where the request page is.
          </p>
          <p>
            If the server is connected to Radarr or Sonarr, approved requests go straight there. You
            decide per user whether they may request at all, and whether their requests need your nod or
            approve themselves. Two people asking for the same film join one request rather than
            creating two.
          </p>
          <p>
            From the viewer's side it just reads as status on the title they wanted: <em>Request
            pending</em>, then <em>Downloading</em>, then <em>Available in library</em> — and an email
            when it lands, if the server sends mail.
          </p>
          <div class="btn-row">
            <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/server#requests">Setting up request servers</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="gallery gallery--two" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
        <Shot src="web/watchlist" url="vora.local/watchlist" alt="The watchlist, which doubles as the request queue" caption="Add to watchlist — and, if you do not own it, that is the request" />
        <Shot src="web/discovery" url="vora.local/discover" alt="Discover, where titles already owned are flagged in library" caption="Discover flags what you already own, so you only ask for what is missing" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="surface" id="household">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="spotlight">
        <div class="spotlight__body">
          <span class="pill">Profiles</span>
          <h3 style="margin-top: 1rem;">One server, everyone's own Vora</h3>
          <p>
            Each profile keeps its own watch state, ratings, recommendations, layout and theme. Parental
            controls pick allowed ratings separately for films, television and music, hide anything
            unrated, and restrict which libraries and IPTV providers a profile can even see.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Shot src="web/profile-parental-controls" url="vora.local/settings" alt="Parental controls with per-rating toggles" />
      </div>

      <div class="spotlight spotlight--reverse">
        <div class="spotlight__body">
          <h3>Watching hours</h3>
          <p>
            Scheduled access windows decide when a profile can sign in at all — useful for school nights,
            and quieter than an argument.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Shot src="web/profile-scheduled-access-times" url="vora.local/settings" alt="Scheduled access times for a profile" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="surface" id="personal">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="spotlight">
        <div class="spotlight__body">
          <span class="pill">Personalize</span>
          <h3 style="margin-top: 1rem;">Your home screen, your palette</h3>
          <p>
            Choose which rows appear on Home and in what order, reorder the navigation itself, and swap
            the client to a different visual template. Templates can even be scheduled, so a seasonal look
            arrives and leaves on its own.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Shot src="web/customize-home-screen" url="vora.local" alt="The home screen row customizer" />
      </div>

      <Shot
        src="web/client-templates"
        url="vora.local/settings"
        alt="The client template picker"
        caption="Client templates — the whole interface recolours, brand mark included"
        class="spotlight-figure"
      />
    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="surface" id="roadmap">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Coming soon</span>
        <h2>Being built now</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Vora is in beta and moving quickly. These are the next things on the bench — no dates, because
          dates on a one-person project are a promise nobody should make.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="roadmap">
        <article>
          <h3>Vora on Google Play</h3>
          <p>
            The Android app is on its way to the Play Store, so installing it on a phone or a television
            stops involving a sideload and starts updating itself.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Ebook libraries</h3>
          <p>
            Books as a first-class library alongside films, television and music — shelves, metadata,
            covers and reading position, handled the same way everything else is.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Custom client templates</h3>
          <p>
            Today you choose from the bundled looks. Next, build your own: pick the colours and drop in
            your own background images, then keep it to yourself or hand it to the household.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Proper multi-server support</h3>
          <p>
            The clients already hold more than one server. The goal is for several servers to feel like
            one library — search, watchlist and continue-watching spanning all of them instead of making
            you remember which box has what.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="doors">
        <a class="door" href="/apps">
          <h3>See it on your devices</h3>
          <p>Web, Android phone, Android TV and Android Auto, screen by screen.</p>
          <span>Every screen &rarr;</span>
        </a>
        <a class="door" href="/server">
          <h3>Run it yourself</h3>
          <p>Libraries, plugins, transcoding, backups and the Plex importer.</p>
          <span>Server &amp; administration &rarr;</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
